#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179639 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eade8768-b97c-5e90-ad02-255b9e8f8dae" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "ae7ee9c8-3162-564e-a3f2-f235b60527f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13903" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2 = wz - 2w +1$ for the variable $w$." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $w$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= wz - 2w + 1 \\\\\n2 - 1 &= wz - 2w + 1 - 1 \\\\\n1 &= wz - 2w\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $w$ in the expression on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= wz - 2w \\\\\n1 &= w(z - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(z-2)$ to isolate $w$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= w(z - 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{z - 2} &= (w(z - 2))/(z - 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{z - 2} &= (w(z - 2))/(z - 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{z - 2} &= w \\\\\nw &= \\frac{1}{z - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\nz-2\\neq 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc2b01b-a60c-53a7-837e-1c6d9a941e98" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations With Variables on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "7f0aa09d-c765-5ab5-ac89-3fb4ce168d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-13905" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4ky=5y + 1$ for the variable $y$." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $5y$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4ky &= 5y + 1 \\\\\n4ky - 5y &= 5y + 1 - 5y \\\\\n4ky - 5y &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $y$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $y$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4ky - 5y &= 1 \\\\\ny(4k - 5) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(4k -5)$ to isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(4k - 5) &= 1 \\\\\n(y(4k - 5))/((4k - 5)) &= (1)/((4k - 5)) \\\\\n(y(4k - 5))/((4k - 5)) &= \\frac{1}{4k - 5} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{4k - 5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\n4k-5\\neq 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b201f4cd-b39c-5ca5-9565-0618859cd15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Using Cross Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "f9e1e805-32a3-5d25-8b09-36707350ab61" :question/math-academy-id "e-13907" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{xy}{3} = \\dfrac{y - 1}{2}$ for the variable $y$." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $y$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{xy}{3} &= \\frac{y - 1}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot xy &= 3 \\cdot (y - 1) \\\\\n2xy &= 3y - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $3y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2xy &= 3y - 3 \\\\\n2xy - 3y &= 3y - 3 - 3y \\\\\n2xy - 3y &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $y$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $y$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $y$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2xy - 3y &= -3 \\\\\ny(2x - 3) &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(2x - 3)$ to isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(2x - 3) &= -3 \\\\\n(y(2x - 3))/(2x - 3) &= \\frac{-3}{2x - 3} \\\\\n(y(2x - 3))/(2x - 3) &= \\frac{-3}{2x - 3} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{2x - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that\n\n$$\n2x-3\\neq 0\n$$"}]}
